 "intro": "Near Fidenae, a few miles up the Tiber from Rome, 437 BC. Fidenae has defected to Veii and murdered Roman envoys. You are Aulus Cornelius Cossus, cavalry commander under the dictator Mamercus Aemilius.",
 "debrief": "In Livy’s account, Cossus charged Lars Tolumnius, king of Veii, unhorsed him with his spear and killed him. The Etruscans broke, and Rome won. Cossus dedicated the king’s armour as the spolia opima, only the second Roman after Romulus to do so. Augustus claimed to have seen the inscribed linen breastplate, which raised doubts about the date. Early Roman history is partly legend. The other choices are what-ifs.",
